Check contact readiness without a settling sleep. Check the ended-call
status, history, direction and duration against measured elapsed time,
instead of assuming that the complete call always takes zero seconds.

Exercise cancellation through ordinary, priority and batched store
calls.

Make the per-user chat-item TTL examples deterministic: use a large ttl
while setting up and asserting, then drop it to 1 so the items expire
synchronously instead of relying on the periodic cleanup and on how long
the (loaded) builder takes. The timed-message example uses a ttl
comfortably larger than the assertions. Give the aborted
connection-switch examples a larger settle delay before reading the
persisted chat items.

The timeout-retry fixture checks Bob's confirmation and connected
notifications in either order: they come from independent command and
subscriber threads. The "subscribed 1 connections" line belongs to
Alice's own setup and is not part of Bob's output.

The client-timeout example assumes that a 10 microsecond transport
timeout always interrupts a loopback SMP command. System.Timeout.timeout
cannot fire below the RTS timer granularity (one setitimer/kqueue tick,
~10 ms on OpenBSD), and getResponse() re-reads the response variable
after the timeout expires, so depending on the machine load the command
either completes first - and the client reports "confirmation sent!" -
or the timeout fires - and it reports the broker error. Both are valid
outcomes of that race, so the example accepts either instead of
asserting the one OpenBSD happened to report.

